course: dictionary
lesson: 1222
title: Bulge: a lump that sticks out
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bulge is a round lump that sticks out from something that is usually flat or smooth. Look at the words around it for clues. In «A can of soda left too long in the freezer can bulge and burst», the can changes shape because something inside is pushing it outwards.
explain: Bulge can also be a verb, which means an action: to stick out because something inside is pushing. In «Rat with his belt bulging with weapons», the belt is so full that it sticks out. Check by swapping in your meaning: "his belt sticking out with weapons" still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Bulge can also mean a sudden increase, as in a bulge in the number of pupils. The sentence around it tells you which meaning the writer wants, so always read on before you decide.
fact: A bulge is a round lump that sticks out from something that is usually flat.
fact: To bulge means to stick out in a round lump because something inside is pushing.
fact: «Bulging eyes» often show surprise or fright, and «bulging pockets» are full of things.
fact: A tin or packet that bulges may mean the food has gone bad, so do not eat it.
fact: «The eastern bulge of Brazil» is a part of the land that sticks out, and it fits into the curve of West Africa like a jigsaw piece.
exercise: «A can of soda left too long in the freezer can bulge and burst.» What does bulge mean here? | Becomes flat and smooth | Freezes solid and turns to ice | *Sticks out in a round lump because something inside is pushing | Makes a loud noise
exercise: Look at «bulging eyes». What do bulging eyes often show? | Tiredness after a long walk | A cold | Boredom in class | *Surprise or fright
exercise: The school has a bulge in the number of pupils this year. What does bulge mean here? | *A sudden increase | A soft, quiet sound | A flat, smooth surface | A kind of sweet
test: «Look at a world map: the bulge of Brazil tucks into the curve of West Africa.» What is a bulge in this sentence? | A bridge over the sea | A map of Africa | A line drawn across the map | *A part of the land that sticks out from the rest
test: A tin of beans is bulging on the shelf. What should you do, and why? | *Do not eat it, because it may have gone bad | Eat it quickly before it spoils | Shake it to make the bulge go down | Put it in the freezer to make it crisp
exam: «The Moon's gravity also tugs the oceans into bulges.» What does bulges mean here? | Still, flat, calm patches of water | *Raised lumps of water that stick out from the rest of the sea | Waves that crash on the beach | Deep holes in the sea floor
